// @test: run
// @tier: 0,1
// @level: L0
// @tags: sys, intrinsic, ffi, libsystem, regression
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-exit: 0
//
// THE CPU COUNT IS THE MACHINE'S OWN (T1459).
//
// `core.sys.num_cpus()` forwards to the `num_cpus` intrinsic, which the AOT
// answers through a platform helper calling the system's own
// `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)`. That helper DECLARED `sysconf` without
// defining it — and a lowering pass that hands a body to every bodyless
// function then gave it `skipped_entry { ret 0 }`, INTERNAL, shadowing the
// real dylib symbol.
//
// Measured on a 16-core machine before the fix:
//
//     via the sys wrapper     tier 0: 16     tier 1: 1
//     nm on the binary        t _sysconf beside libsystem_c's
//     its whole body          mov x0, #0 ; ret
//
// The 1 is a clamp lifting 0, so nothing looked wrong: no crash, no error,
// a plausible number. `core/runtime/config.vr` sizes its worker pool from
// this call, so an AOT binary ran ONE worker on sixteen cores.
//
// WHAT THIS SPEC CAN AND CANNOT ASSERT. The true count differs per machine,
// so a constant would pin the wrong thing. What holds everywhere is that the
// two tiers AGREE and that the count is plausible — at least one, and not
// the clamp's floor masquerading as an answer on a machine that has more.
// The second rung is the one that fails when the symbol is shadowed again:
// a shadowed `sysconf` answers 0 for every caller, so the two routes to the
// same number stop agreeing.
//
// The `+ 0` on the assertions is deliberate: a bare `assert(n >= 1)` can be
// folded by the frontend's own model.

mount core.sys.num_cpus;

fn main() {
    print("0 begin");

    let via_wrapper = num_cpus();
    let via_intrinsic = core.intrinsics.num_cpus();

    print(f"1 wrapper   = {via_wrapper + 0} (want >= 1)");
    assert(via_wrapper + 0 >= 1);

    print(f"2 agree     = {(via_wrapper == via_intrinsic)} (want true)");
    assert(via_wrapper == via_intrinsic);

    print("3 end");
}
